<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    SUPPORT-646 (Stage 7 bugfix): bruteForceLoginProtectionAdmin is declared as a TOP-LEVEL
    permission node (sibling of the shared "admin" category), NOT nested under <admin>.

    Investigation note (see 07-bugfix-report.md for full evidence): the ACTUAL confirmed root
    cause of the "module-only role denied" bug turned out to be a MISSING j:privilegedAccess="true"
    on the role in roles.xml (see that file), not this nesting. Reading Jahia's own privilege
    registration code (JahiaPrivilegeRegistry.registerPrivileges) shows a nested jnt:permission
    node is still registered as its own independent, standalone JCR privilege keyed by its own
    local name, so nesting under <admin> does not, by itself, block standalone grantability.

    This restructuring to a top-level node is kept anyway as defense-in-depth / cleaner
    module ownership: the pre-existing <admin> node in this file is Jahia core's own shared
    "server settings" permission-category node (see WEB-INF/etc/repository/root-permissions.xml
    in jahia-war), reused (not duplicated) by JCR import because it already exists at that path.
    Nesting our own custom permission inside a node core Jahia and other modules also own is
    unnecessary coupling for a module-owned permission that has no real parent/child relationship
    with "admin" — hoisting it to the top level removes that shared-ownership risk even though it
    was not the cause of the bug fixed in roles.xml.
-->
<permissions xmlns:jcr="http://www.jcp.org/jcr/1.0">
    <bruteForceLoginProtectionAdmin jcr:primaryType="jnt:permission"/>
</permissions>
